claimed: json-parse-fast-road -- found while measuring py-arrow, and it
is not what py-arrow said it was.

py-arrow is filed as "frames via pyarrow, once the JSON-frame road
hurts". It hurts: a 500k-row frame round-trips through okay-py in 9.7
seconds. But the measurement says WHERE, and it is not Python and not
the wire:

  500k rows, 19.7 MB   round trip 9.7 s
                       scala encode      0.2 s
                       Json.parse        4.9 s   <- 60% of everything
                       our own walk      0.03 s
                       Json.parseValue   0.06 s  <- 79x faster, EQUAL value

`Json.parse` is the LOSSLESS road: tokenize to a full CST, then
project. `Json.parseValue` is one strict pass that falls back to the
lossless road when it is not sure, so it is total and TestJsonValue
already asserts the two agree. Codecs.readJson -- the generic JSON
decode door the whole stack uses -- takes the slow road.

So: audit every Json.parse on a hot path, move it to parseValue where
no caller needs the CST, and measure. py-arrow gets re-filed with an
honest justification, because the number that motivated it was mostly
our own parser.

Files: okay-codec Codecs.scala, the hot-path callers (okay-py,
okay-r, okay-mcp, okay-llm, okay-agent, okay-http consumers...),
tests, specs/codecs.md, boards.
